    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d280">

                <pb facs="0386" n="268" sameAs="frus:frus1929v03/pg268"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">694.113Lumber/122: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of
                            State to the Chargé in Japan</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Neville</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1929-11-27T15:00:00-05:00">November 27, 1929—3
                        p.m.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p>120. Your 110, November 26, 5 p.m. Your proposed action approved.<anchor n="45"
                        xml:id="d280-f1"/></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Cotton</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d280-f1" n="45" xml:id="d280-f1-content">
                <p>In despatch No. 165, March 17, 1931, the Chargé in Japan reported to the
                    Secretary of State the action taken by the Japanese Government on March 12 to
                    revise the lumber tariff by increasing Siberian and Asiatic mainland import
                    duties (694.113 Lumber/129). The Japanese Diet subsequently passed the
                    proposal.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
